  "units": [
    {
      "id": "1",
      "tag": "h1",
      "sourceHTML": "Syntax",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Syntax",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Wren’s syntax is designed to be familiar to people coming from C-like languages\nwhile being a bit simpler and more streamlined.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Wren’s syntax is designed to be familiar to people coming from C-like languages\nwhile being a bit simpler and more streamlined.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Scripts are stored in plain text files with a ⟦CODE0⟧ file extension. Wren does\nnot compile ahead of time: programs are run directly from source, from top to\nbottom like a typical scripting language. (Internally, programs are compiled to\nbytecode for <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/22-performance/\">efficiency</a>, but that’s an implementation detail.)",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Scripts are stored in plain text files with a <code>.wren</code> file extension. Wren does\nnot compile ahead of time: programs are run directly from source, from top to\nbottom like a typical scripting language. (Internally, programs are compiled to\nbytecode for <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/22-performance/\">efficiency</a>, but that’s an implementation detail.)",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>.wren</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Comments <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#comments\" name=\"comments\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Comments <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#comments\" name=\"comments\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Line comments start with ⟦CODE0⟧ and end at the end of the line:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Line comments start with <code>//</code> and end at the end of the line:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>//</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Block comments start with ⟦CODE0⟧ and end with ⟦CODE1⟧. They can span multiple lines:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Block comments start with <code>/*</code> and end with <code>*/</code>. They can span multiple lines:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>/*</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE1⟧",
          "html": "<code>*/</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Unlike C, block comments can nest in Wren:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Unlike C, block comments can nest in Wren:",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "This is handy because it lets you easily comment out an entire block of code,\neven if the code already contains block comments.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "This is handy because it lets you easily comment out an entire block of code,\neven if the code already contains block comments.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Reserved words <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#reserved-words\" name=\"reserved-words\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Reserved words <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#reserved-words\" name=\"reserved-words\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "One way to get a quick feel for a language’s style is to see what words it\nreserves. Here’s what Wren has:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "One way to get a quick feel for a language’s style is to see what words it\nreserves. Here’s what Wren has:",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Identifiers <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#identifiers\" name=\"identifiers\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Identifiers <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#identifiers\" name=\"identifiers\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Naming rules are similar to other programming languages. Identifiers start with\na letter or underscore and may contain letters, digits, and underscores. Case\nis sensitive.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Naming rules are similar to other programming languages. Identifiers start with\na letter or underscore and may contain letters, digits, and underscores. Case\nis sensitive.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "13",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Identifiers that start with underscore (⟦CODE0⟧) are special in Wren. They are used\nto indicate <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/10-classes/#fields\">fields</a> in classes.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Identifiers that start with underscore (<code>_</code>) are special in Wren. They are used\nto indicate <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/10-classes/#fields\">fields</a> in classes.",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>_</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "14",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Newlines <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#newlines\" name=\"newlines\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Newlines <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#newlines\" name=\"newlines\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "15",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Newlines (⟦CODE0⟧) are meaningful in Wren. They are used to separate statements:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Newlines (<code>\\n</code>) are meaningful in Wren. They are used to separate statements:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>\\n</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "16",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Sometimes, though, a statement doesn’t fit on a single line and jamming a\nnewline in the middle would trip it up. To handle that, Wren has a very simple\nrule: It ignores a newline following any token that can’t end a statement.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Sometimes, though, a statement doesn’t fit on a single line and jamming a\nnewline in the middle would trip it up. To handle that, Wren has a very simple\nrule: It ignores a newline following any token that can’t end a statement.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "17",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "In practice, this means you can put each statement on its own line and wrap\nthem across lines as needed without too much trouble.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "In practice, this means you can put each statement on its own line and wrap\nthem across lines as needed without too much trouble.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "18",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Blocks <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#blocks\" name=\"blocks\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Blocks <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#blocks\" name=\"blocks\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "19",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Wren uses curly braces to define <em>blocks</em>. You can use a block anywhere a\nstatement is allowed, like in <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/08-control-flow/\">control flow</a> statements.\n<a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/10-classes/#methods\">Method</a> and <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/11-functions/\">function</a> bodies are also\nblocks. For example, here we have a block for the then case, and a single\nstatement for the else:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Wren uses curly braces to define <em>blocks</em>. You can use a block anywhere a\nstatement is allowed, like in <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/08-control-flow/\">control flow</a> statements.\n<a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/10-classes/#methods\">Method</a> and <a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/11-functions/\">function</a> bodies are also\nblocks. For example, here we have a block for the then case, and a single\nstatement for the else:",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "20",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Blocks have two similar but not identical forms. Typically, blocks contain a\nseries of statements like:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Blocks have two similar but not identical forms. Typically, blocks contain a\nseries of statements like:",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "21",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Blocks of this form when used for method and function bodies automatically\nreturn ⟦CODE0⟧ after the block has completed. If you want to return a different\nvalue, you need an explicit ⟦CODE1⟧ statement.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Blocks of this form when used for method and function bodies automatically\nreturn <code>null</code> after the block has completed. If you want to return a different\nvalue, you need an explicit <code>return</code> statement.",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>null</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE1⟧",
          "html": "<code>return</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "22",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "However, it’s pretty common to have a method or function that just evaluates and\nreturns the result of a single expression. Some other languages use ⟦CODE0⟧ to\ndefine these. Wren uses:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "However, it’s pretty common to have a method or function that just evaluates and\nreturns the result of a single expression. Some other languages use <code>=&gt;</code> to\ndefine these. Wren uses:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>=&gt;</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "23",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "If there is no newline after the ⟦CODE0⟧ (or after the parameter list in a\n<a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/11-functions/\">function</a>), then the block may only contain a single\nexpression, and it automatically returns the result of it. It’s exactly the same\nas doing:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "If there is no newline after the <code>{</code> (or after the parameter list in a\n<a href=\"/docs/wren/v0-4-0/en/01-guide/11-functions/\">function</a>), then the block may only contain a single\nexpression, and it automatically returns the result of it. It’s exactly the same\nas doing:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>{</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "24",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Statements are not allowed in this form (since they don’t produce values), which\nmeans nothing starting with ⟦CODE0⟧, ⟦CODE1⟧, ⟦CODE2⟧, ⟦CODE3⟧,  ⟦CODE4⟧,\n⟦CODE5⟧, or ⟦CODE6⟧. If you want a block that contains a single statement,\nput a newline in there:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Statements are not allowed in this form (since they don’t produce values), which\nmeans nothing starting with <code>class</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>import</code>,  <code>return</code>,\n<code>var</code>, or <code>while</code>. If you want a block that contains a single statement,\nput a newline in there:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>class</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE1⟧",
          "html": "<code>for</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE2⟧",
          "html": "<code>if</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE3⟧",
          "html": "<code>import</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE4⟧",
          "html": "<code>return</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE5⟧",
          "html": "<code>var</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE6⟧",
          "html": "<code>while</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "25",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "Using an initial newline after the ⟦CODE0⟧ does feel a little weird or magical, but\nnewlines are already significant in Wren, so it’s not totally unreasonable. The nice\nthing about this syntax as opposed to something like ⟦CODE1⟧ is that the <em>end</em> of\nthe block has an explicit delimiter. That helps when chaining:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Using an initial newline after the <code>{</code> does feel a little weird or magical, but\nnewlines are already significant in Wren, so it’s not totally unreasonable. The nice\nthing about this syntax as opposed to something like <code>=&gt;</code> is that the <em>end</em> of\nthe block has an explicit delimiter. That helps when chaining:",
      "protected": [
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE0⟧",
          "html": "<code>{</code>"
        },
        {
          "token": "⟦CODE1⟧",
          "html": "<code>=&gt;</code>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "26",
      "tag": "h2",
      "sourceHTML": "Precedence and Associativity <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#precedence-and-associativity\" name=\"precedence-and-associativity\">#</a>",
      "originalInnerHTML": "Precedence and Associativity <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#precedence-and-associativity\" name=\"precedence-and-associativity\">#</a>",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "27",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "We’ll talk about Wren’s different expression forms and what they mean in the\nnext few pages. But if you want to see how they interact with each other\ngrammatically, here’s the whole table.",
      "originalInnerHTML": "We’ll talk about Wren’s different expression forms and what they mean in the\nnext few pages. But if you want to see how they interact with each other\ngrammatically, here’s the whole table.",
      "protected": []
    },
    {
      "id": "28",
      "tag": "p",
      "sourceHTML": "It shows which expressions have higher <em>precedence</em>—which ones bind more\ntightly than others—and their <em>associativity</em>—how a series of the\nsame kind of expression is ordered. Wren mostly follows C, except that it fixes\n<a href=\"http://www.lysator.liu.se/c/dmr-on-or.html\">the bitwise operator mistake</a>. The full precedence table, from\ntightest to loosest, is:",
      "originalInnerHTML": "It shows which expressions have higher <em>precedence</em>—which ones bind more\ntightly than others—and their <em>associativity</em>—how a series of the\nsame kind of expression is ordered. Wren mostly follows C, except that it fixes\n<a href=\"http://www.lysator.liu.se/c/dmr-on-or.html\">the bitwise operator mistake</a>. The full precedence table, from\ntightest to loosest, is:",
      "protected": []
